ly compounds in you know  in remote places where nobody's allowed  to go and and they they help one another  they invest with one another there they  do business together they socialize  together by preference and they make  careful marriages as well but also at  the lower-class level among  working-class and immigrant communities  nepotism  is still the way things are done and  again you know one of the prejudices  that people have about nepotism is that  it's all about class privilege it's  somehow you know nepotism is considered  to be a disease of the rich and that the  only people who practice it are wealthy  people who are we're allowed to buy  privileges for their children but the  truth is quite quite the opposite that  that nepotism is the traditional way in  which working-class people and  particularly immigrant communities have  pulled together and helped helped one  another and middle-class people in this  country have by and large conveniently  forgotten that their position in the  middle class as doctors or lawyers or  engineers or what-have-you is generally  owed to two or three generations of  ethnic nepotism can you remember when  you were conscious of your father as a  writer where and then can you remember  where you decided I'm gonna be a writer  oh sure I was very young and that's  really all I knew about my father really  because I didn't live with him when I  was five six seven years old I wanted to  know everybody else had a daddy and I  wanted to know they all you know they're  better that we've come to school and  they would say what their fathers did I  didn't really didn't know what my father  did and I had to ask my mother and she  said your father's a writer well I  didn't really know what that meant  but it's overtime of course I became  more aware of it I think very early on I  decided that I wanted to be a writer and  I think it's very common for children of  accomplished parents to just to make  that choice because what I find in  reading the accounts that various  successors today give of their motives  for going into the family business is  that they want to be close to their  parents very very often as you know  successful and accomplished people are  often distant and absent and distracted  and preoccupied and so the children  really have a psychological need to to  get their parents attention and and they  often choose to do this by following in  their footsteps by the way you saw  Bethel wrote what are some of the big  names of the knowledge erode well his  his first really famous novel was The  Adventures of Augie March which was  published I believe 1953 before my  before I was born  Henderson the rain King Hertzog are his  some of his other better known books and  most recently his last novel was called  Ravel Stein which was a portrait of the  late Allan bloom was a very close friend  of it now have you ever had anybody  analyzed the way you write in the way he  writes well no I have not  but I I it may it may happen I I'm  conscious of certain echoes of my  father's prose style and but not just  his prose style but his way of speaking  give us an example well he has a way of  well first of all I guess what you're  really asking me is is how much like my  father am I or the opposite of that how  much different are you well I think  you'd have to ask my wife in a way to  for that the answer to that question I  mean one difference between us is that  I've been married for 16 or 17 years and  my father has had five marriages so  there's clearly a difference there and  in fact all both of my brothers are are  committed monogamists whereas our father  was was known for for having liaisons  and girlfriends so I'm really actually  talking about the writing part of it  though yeah I mean I know you know I'm  you're watching this a little bit I'm  not sure how to answer your question I  think this is a certain kind of tonality  and I mean look at it this way my father  came from a immigrant impoverished  immigrant environment multilingual he  grew up in the in his home speaking  Yiddish and Russian and English and some  French because the family lived in  Montreal and this developed allowed  these sources allowed him out of these  sources he developed a very distinctive  voice it's it's ironic it's sort of you  know Jewish but also high-faluting it's  until very intellectual so this is the  kind of marriage of high and low that  you find in his prose and give so much  energy to his writing I also the the wit  is he's famous he's very very funny both  on the page and in person and I grew up  in an environment where we're we're  literacy literary literary education and  wit a certain kind of earthy puncturing  penetrating wit were very valued so I  learned to to use language in a way from  him that I think I wouldn't have learned  from anybody else of all the characters  you wrote about in the book is there  anybody that you got interested enough  and then you might expand on it later or  if you don't do that is who was your  favorite character well I think my  favorite character in this book is the  father of Napoleon Bonaparte a man who  has been I think unfairly reviled and  his bad reputation begins with Napoleon  himself and as I write in the book that  the section devoted to the Bonaparte  family is really about Napoleon's father  because Napoleon is considered to be the  great self-made man of them  Neera you know the example of a young  fatherless young man who isn't even  really French comes from from Corsica  rises through the mill of French  military to the heights of political  power brings his brothers and sisters  along with him of course and takes care  of them but he always a Napoleon always  ascribed his success it's pretty much to  his own genius and ability and what I  found when I looked into the the story  of the Bonaparte family is that  Napoleon's father Carlo Bonaparte a was  really underestimated by his own  children and I think you know in a sad  and ungrateful way this is the story of  a man who really devoted his entire life  to getting his children into the system  of French nobility we just have a moment  left and I have to ask you about this  cover whose idea was it and for those  that you can't tell her that's that's a  raised silver spoon well I was not part  of this discussion  you know authors are not invited to  participate in jacket meetings so I  don't know exactly whose idea it was but  I do know that there was some difficulty  figuring out how to illustrate this book  and somebody I think it was Bill Thomas  the editorial director at Doubleday said  well something this is really about  inherited privilege and isn't it  commonly said that you know the people  are people with inherited privileges are  born with the silver spoon in their  mouth and everybody seemed to think that  was a wonderful idea and the resulting  jacket I think is one of the one of the  most beautiful striking and elegant  jackets I've ever seen in publishing  that's our time and this is again what  the cover looks like and our guest has  been Adam Bello the name of this book is  in praise of nepotism thank you very  much thank you Brian
 

